WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.110
Meet YieldShield, built by David Hawig and
Santi.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.020
David previously served as Director of
Ecosystem at Web3 Foundation and founded a

3
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:13.580
startup. The team is starting with MON and
shMON holders who want to

4
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.210
keep crypto exposure while understanding their
choices when prices fall.

5
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.750
YieldShield offers two exits.

6
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.150
Keep the remaining asset, or surrender it for
an eligible backing payout.

7
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:30.450
Other participants supply the backing, share
realized gains, and take first-loss risk.

8
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.250
Caps, fees and waiting periods apply.

9
00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.190
The Monad testnet application has wallet
onboarding and seven funded test pools.

10
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.670
Recorded transactions demonstrate holder and
provider actions.

11
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.170
The test assets have no monetary value; no
customer adoption or revenue is claimed.

12
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.710
YieldShield predates Metropolis. The protocol
foundation is reused and disclosed.

13
00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:59.480
The Monad application, integrations and
deployment are the new milestone, with AI

14
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:04.580
assistance documented publicly. Next, the team
plans to observe six holders and four

15
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.990
potential backing providers. Can holders
explain the exits?

16
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.270
Do providers accept the economics?

17
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.750
Do people return? Evidence from both sides
should guide a real-funds launch.
